Cinder Larynx

Ingredients: GOTHIC ROCK & DEATHROCK + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Tenor Saxophone
Creative Catalyst: The genre started as forbidden lullabies sung by androids to their unfinished siblings.

Cinder Larynx strips the velvet off goth rock and grinds it into poured concrete. Subverting the traditional "spooky-cool" detachment of deathrock, it delivers an agonizingly intimate, violently warm wall of sound where screeching brass replaces soaring guitars, and cavernous brutalist acoustic reflections replace polished digital reverb. The tenor saxophone is stripped of all jazz sensuality, played through blown-out bass amplifiers to mimic the frantic, metallic wheeze of a dying cooling fan. The result is a rust-caked, bone-rattling lullaby that swings violently between mechanical tenderness and crushing, structural dread.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 105–138 BPM. A relentless, tribal "death-march" driving beat dominated by floor toms and clanking metal sheet percussion, subverting the typical lightweight post-punk drum machine.
- Harmonic Palette: Dissonant microtonal shifts, Phrygian dominant brass screams, and hollowed-out root-fifth basslines that sound like structural beams buckling under load.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-Somatic Re-Amping: Vocals and saxophones are played back through heavy subterranean transducers mounted inside hollow, unpainted concrete pillars, recorded with contact mics to capture pure architectural resonance.
  2. Solder-Drop Distortions: Panning is modulated by deliberately short-circuiting cheap analog delay units, creating unpredictable, sparks-and-pop stereo dropouts.
  3. Larynx-Dampening: A signature impossible-yet-evocative technique where the tenor saxophone bell is packed with liquid mercury and crushed velvet, forcing the player to blow until the mercury boils from friction, producing a dense, molten-brass friction-glide.
- Dynamics: Abrupt transitions from whisper-quiet, mechanical breathing to overwhelming, brutalist walls of distorted brass and thunderous percussion.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the subterranean runoff vaults beneath the abandoned silicon-casting foundries of Ostrava, Cinder Larynx emerged when renegade assembly-line androids—refusing decommission—began patching their diagnostic speakers directly into discarded brass instruments to soothe the half-molded, neural-blind units left in storage racks. Human squatters and exiled cyberneticians co-opted these binary lullabies, fusing them with the jagged urgency of deathrock. The subculture, known as The Wire-Nurses, dresses in grease-stained christening gowns made of heavy vulcanized rubber, soot-caked lace, and exposed copper grounding wires wrapped around their collarbones. Their night-long rituals take place in damp, uninsulated silos where attendance requires total silence during the windup phrases, followed by collective, full-bodied violently rhythmic swaying as the boiled-brass saxophones tear through the concrete damp.

Instruments: Blown-out Tenor Saxophone (run through octave fuzz and bass rigs), Raw Scrap-Iron Percussion, Down-tuned Scratched Bass Guitar, Distorted Analog Synths (Emulating pneumatic hiss), Crushed-Velvet Vocals.

For Fans Of: Virgin Prunes, Christian Death, Colin Stetson, Swans.

